Settle player identity across interfaces. `bluos-api/SPEC-ADDITIONS-identity.md`
says a player has two interfaces, two MACs and two IPs, only one live at a
time, and that which one is live changes on its own. Three things are open
there:

- The test, which answers both open claims in one run: on a player that has
  run both ways, record `/SyncStatus` `id` and `mac` plus the LSDP `nodeId` on
  Ethernet, pull the cable, let it join wireless, and repeat. That says whether
  `mac` follows the live interface and whether `nodeId` is stable.
- The vendor pages behind the hardware behaviour were read through search
  result summaries, not opened — support1.bluesound.com is unreachable from
  the machine that file was written on. Open them and check the wording before
  any of it is relied on.
- `C-56-syncstatus-mac-interface` and `C-57-lsdp-nodeid-interface-stable` exist
  only as proposals in that file; the register in `bluos-http-api.md` still
  ends at C-55. Fold them in once the test and the sources are done.

This one matters more than it looks: anything that dedupes players on `id`, or
on whatever address it reached a player at, turns one player into two the first
time somebody moves it between wired and wireless, and keeps a stale entry it
can never reach again.

