Consistency sweep of the whole bluos-api corpus, to be done as one task rather
than in passing. The known inconsistencies are listed below, but the point of
the task is the sweep, not the list: assume more exist, because every one below
was found by accident while looking for something else.

Known:

- `C-03`, above.
- The "Resolved on hardware" summary table says role reversal is "Rejected as
  a cycle; original master reasserts". §5.3 disproves exactly that, a few
  sections earlier, with the mutual master loop.
- §17 carries two claim tables, topic-ordered and id-ordered, maintained by
  hand. `C-03` and `C-04` already show them drifting.
- A dozen claims resolved in the hardware `FINDINGS.md` never reached §17.1 or
  the body: `C-07` through `C-15`, `C-18`, `C-22`, `C-52`.
- `C-56` and `C-57` exist only in `SPEC-ADDITIONS-identity.md`; the register
  ends at `C-55`.
- `PLAN.md` has its own R1-R6 requirement list that is a different set from
  `MOTIVATION.md`'s R1-R8, and the numbers collide rather than simply
  disagreeing: PLAN's R3 is sorting, which is MOTIVATION's R5; PLAN's R1 is
  the GUI, which is MOTIVATION's R3; PLAN's R6 has no counterpart at all.
  `DESIGN_PRINCIPLES.md` says "R3 exists because of it" about sorting, so
  following that reference into MOTIVATION lands on the wrong requirement.
  Known and deliberately left alone so far — it still has to be settled before
  either document is relied on, and `README.md` is the authority.

How to sweep, rather than eyeballing it:

- Every claim id in §17 and §17.1, against each other and against the
  `FINDINGS.md` of every run in `test-runs/`. Flag any id whose verdict
  differs between the two tables, any id tested in a run but absent from the
  register, and any verdict whose evidence was redacted out of the repo —
  `REDACTIONS.md` in each run says what left.
- Every summary table against the section it cites. They are hand-written
  digests of body text and nothing keeps them in step.
- `SPEC-ADDITIONS-*.md` and `SPEC-SECTION-*.md` against the spec, to find
  proposals silently superseded by later edits as well as ones never merged.
- Cross-document references by symbol: `R<n>`, `C-<n>`, `D<n>`, `§<n>`. Resolve
  each one and check it points at what the sentence claims. That is what caught
  the R-numbering collision.

